      "html": "<ul><li>Synonym: Liobagrus xiurenensis</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amblycipitidae/xiurenbagrus_xiurenensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>Asia: China, Zhujiang River. The species is not well known beyond its type locality, the Xiuren River, Pearl River drainage, Guangxi Province, China.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amblycipitidae/xiurenbagrus_xiurenensis.htm",
      "html": "<p>There are three species in this genus: X. dorsalis, X. xiurenensis and X. gigas. Dorsal spines (total): 1; dorsal soft rays (total): 6; anal soft rays: 7–8; vertebrae: 41–44. The body is elongated, round in front and gradually flattened towards the back, covered by a membrane. The dorsal fin is short; the eyes are very small and covered by a skin membrane. Colouration: the edges of the dorsal, anal and caudal fins are yellowish; the back is brownish and the belly yellowish.</p><p>Etymology: the specific name xiurenensis has the suffix -ensis, denoting place: Xiuren River, Pearl River drainage, Guangxi Province, China, the type locality.</p><p>IUCN Red List: assessed as Data Deficient as the species is not well known beyond its type locality. Potential threats include overfishing and pollution.</p>"
    }
  ],
